\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\kerning2\b\f0\fs24\lang16393 LangGraph and LangChain\par
LangChain\par
LangChain\b0  is an open-source framework designed to simplify the development of applications powered by Large Language Models (LLMs). It provides a set of tools, abstractions, and components that help developers build AI systems capable of interacting with external data, performing reasoning tasks, and executing actions. Instead of directly calling an LLM through an API, LangChain provides a structured approach for creating complete AI applications that combine prompts, models, memory, tools, and retrieval systems.\par
At its core, LangChain enables developers to create pipelines that connect various AI components together. A typical workflow starts with loading documents, splitting them into manageable chunks, converting those chunks into vector embeddings, storing them in a vector database, retrieving relevant information based on user queries, and finally generating responses using an LLM. This architecture serves as the foundation for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems.\par
\b Core Components of LangChain\par
1. Model Interface\par
\b0 The model layer provides standardized access to various LLMs such as GPT-4, Claude, Gemini, Llama, and Mistral. Developers can switch between models with minimal code changes.\par
\b 2. Prompt Templates\par
\b0 Prompt templates allow dynamic construction of prompts by inserting variables into predefined structures, ensuring consistency and reusability.\par
\b 3. Chains\par
\b0 A chain connects multiple operations into a single workflow. For example:\par
\b User Query \f1\u8594?\f0\lang16393  Retrieval \f1\u8594?\f0\lang16393  Context Injection \f1\u8594?\f0\lang16393  LLM Response\b0\par
Each component's output becomes the input for the next component.\par
\b 4. Memory\par
\b0 Memory enables applications to retain context from previous interactions. This helps build conversational agents that can remember earlier messages and maintain coherent multi-turn discussions.\par
\b 5. Tools\par
\b0 Tools allow LLMs to interact with external systems such as:\par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Databases\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}APIs\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Search engines\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Calculators\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Enterprise applications\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 This transforms the model from a text generator into an intelligent assistant capable of performing actions.\par
\b 6. Agents\par
\b0 Agents use reasoning to determine which tools should be used and in what order. Instead of following a predefined workflow, agents dynamically decide the next action based on the current objective and available information.\par
