\b Agent Lifecycle\par
\b0 The \b Agent Lifecycle\b0  refers to the sequence of stages an AI agent goes through while perceiving its environment, reasoning about a task, taking actions, and learning from outcomes. Unlike traditional AI models that only generate responses, agentic systems operate continuously, making decisions and interacting with tools, systems, users, and external environments to achieve specific goals.\par
At a high level, an AI agent follows a cyclical process where it receives an objective, gathers information, plans its actions, executes tasks, evaluates results, and improves future performance based on feedback. This iterative nature enables agents to handle complex, multi-step workflows with minimal human intervention.\par
\b 1. Goal Initialization\par
\b0 The lifecycle begins when the agent receives a goal, instruction, or trigger event. The objective can be provided by a user, another agent, a software system, or an external event.\par
Examples:\par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 "Generate a monthly sales report."\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}"Book a meeting with all stakeholders."\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}"Investigate suspicious financial transactions."\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 During this stage, the agent interprets the intent, identifies requirements, understands constraints, and defines success criteria.\par
\par
\b 2. Perception and Context Gathering\par
\b0 Once the objective is understood, the agent collects relevant information from its environment. This process is called perception.\par
The agent may gather data from:\par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Documents and databases\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}APIs and web services\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Sensors or IoT devices\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Enterprise applications\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}User conversations\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Memory stores\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 The collected information forms the context required for decision-making. The richer the context, the better the quality of the agent's reasoning.\par
\par
\b 3. Reasoning and Planning\par
\b0 After gathering data, the agent analyzes the available information and determines the best strategy to achieve the goal.\par
Reasoning involves:\par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Understanding relationships between information\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Evaluating possible actions\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Selecting optimal approaches\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Managing constraints and dependencies\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Planning may include breaking a complex objective into smaller subtasks.\par
For example, a travel-planning agent might decompose the task into:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent360\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Search flights\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}Compare prices\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}Check hotel availability\par
{\pntext\f0 4.\tab}Create itinerary\par
{\pntext\f0 5.\tab}Present recommendations\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Modern agent frameworks such as LangGraph, AutoGen, and CrewAI often use advanced planning mechanisms to coordinate these workflows.\par
\par
\b 4. Tool Selection and Action Execution\par
\b0 Agents become significantly more powerful when they can interact with external tools.\par
During this stage, the agent chooses appropriate tools and performs actions such as:\par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Querying databases\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Calling APIs\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Executing code\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Sending emails\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Updating applications\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Creating reports\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Triggering workflows\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 This stage transforms reasoning into real-world execution. The ability to act is what distinguishes agentic AI from traditional language models.\par
\par
\b 5. Observation and Feedback Collection\par
\b0 After executing actions, the agent observes the outcomes.\par
It validates whether:\par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 The task was completed successfully\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Errors occurred\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Additional information is needed\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}The goal remains partially fulfilled\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Feedback can come from:\par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 System responses\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Human users\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Evaluation metrics\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Monitoring dashboards\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Other collaborating agents\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 This stage helps the agent understand the consequences of its actions.\par
\par
\b 6. Reflection and Evaluation\par
\b0 The agent then assesses its own performance and compares outcomes against the original objective.\par
Questions commonly addressed include:\par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Was the goal achieved?\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Were resources used efficiently?\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Could a better strategy have been applied?\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Were there reasoning errors?\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Reflection enables agents to identify weaknesses and improve future decisions. Modern agent architectures often implement self-reflection loops to enhance reliability and reduce hallucinations.\par
\par
\b 7. Learning and Memory Update\par
\b0 The final stage involves storing useful knowledge gained during execution.\par
The agent updates:\par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Short-term memory (current task context)\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Long-term memory (historical experiences)\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Knowledge bases\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Vector databases\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Behavioral policies\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Learning allows the agent to perform future tasks more effectively by leveraging previous experiences and outcomes.\par
\b Use Cases of the Agent Lifecycle\par
\b0 The Agent Lifecycle (Perceive \f1\u8594?\f0\lang16393  Reason \f1\u8594?\f0\lang16393  Plan \f1\u8594?\f0\lang16393  Act \f1\u8594?\f0\lang16393  Observe \f1\u8594?\f0\lang16393  Learn) is applicable wherever autonomous decision-making and execution are required.\par
